  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Also called plage du Port, Plage Vespins is lined with umbrella pines, supervised in summer and open to swimming for humans and dogs alike, according to the Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office. A large car park, the harbour's, is nearby. Free access, open every day.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The town of Saint-Laurent-du-Var states that only one of its public beaches allows dogs: Vespins (called "limite de commune" in the Regional Health Agency's records). The tourist office says dogs are allowed on the whole beach but must swim in an area delimited by two posts at the end of the beach.</p>
      <p>Leash: the tourist office writes "off-leash" ("en liberté"), while a France 3 article (June 2023) speaks of dogs kept on a leash in the marked area. We found no public municipal order settling it: keep your dog under control and pick up after it.</p>
      <p>Water quality: on the town's table, Plage Vespins is open for swimming, but its 2025 ARS rating is "insufficient" (like the commune's other open beaches, located at the mouth of the Var). The town closes swimming after each insufficient check, or as a precaution in case of an alert or rain; check the day's status before letting your dog swim. Facilities listed by the tourist office: public toilets, showers, lifeguard post. Firefighter lifeguards were on duty from 26 June to 31 August in 2025; 2026 dates not read.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: this is the only beach in the commune where your dog is admitted; swimming is in the area between the two posts.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Saint-Laurent-du-Var harbour and its waterfront restaurants are right next door, beside the tourist information office (527 promenade Alain Gerbault).</p>
